// "our family" content from apps/app/public/family (generated)
window.IH_FAMILY = {"kids":[{"id":"hin-01","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why does Ganesha have an elephant head?","answer":"Hindus tell a story. Goddess Parvati made a boy and asked him to guard her door. Lord Shiva didn't know him, and there was a big mix-up. To make it right, Shiva gave the boy a brand-new head, an elephant's, and made him the one Hindus pray to first. What do you think the big ears are for?","grownup":"The best-known telling is in the Shiva Purana; the Brahmavaivarta Purana tells it differently. Ganesha is honored first at prayers and new beginnings as remover of obstacles. Meanings given to his features (big ears, small eyes) are popular teaching, not scripture.","source":"Shiva Purana, Rudra Samhita, Kumara Khanda (paraphrase); Brahmavaivarta Purana, Ganapati Khanda (variant)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: 'everyone prays to' narrowed to 'Hindus pray to' (no sweeping claim). Shiva Purana, Rudra Samhita, Kumara Khanda checked."}},{"id":"hin-02","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why do we light the diya?","answer":"Many Hindus light a diya to say: light is stronger than darkness. On Diwali, many tell a popular story: Lord Rama came home, and people lit lamps to welcome him. Every day, the little flame is a way to say hello to God and ask for a good heart. Want to light one together for someone who needs some light?","grownup":"Lamps are offered in daily puja and aarti. Diwali stories vary by region: Rama's return to Ayodhya, Krishna's victory over Narakasura, Lakshmi puja. An old Upanishadic prayer asks to be led from darkness to light (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28).","source":"Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28 (paraphrase); Diwali's lamps for Rama's return are popular and regional tradition","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Lamps lit for Rama's homecoming is widely told but not in Valmiki's text as such; confirm framing. Fact-check 2026-10-14: Rama's lamps now labeled a popular story; 'Hindus' softened to 'many Hindus'. BU 1.3.28 (tamaso ma jyotir gamaya) confirmed."}},{"id":"hin-03","path":"HINDUISM","q":"is God a boy or a girl?","answer":"Many Hindus say God is bigger than boy or girl. Sometimes Hindus pray to God as a mother, like Durga or Lakshmi, and sometimes as a father, like Shiva or Vishnu. One picture even shows Shiva as half man, half woman! Hindus say God can be loved in whatever way helps your heart. How do you picture God?","grownup":"Many Hindu schools speak of Brahman, ultimate reality beyond form and gender, worshiped through many forms. Shakta traditions honor the Divine Mother (Devi Mahatmya). Ardhanarishvara, Shiva joined with Parvati in one body, shows masculine and feminine as one.","source":"Devi Mahatmya (Markandeya Purana); Ardhanarishvara iconography; Upanishadic teaching on Brahman (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"hin-04","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why are there so many gods?","answer":"A very old Hindu verse says: truth is one, and wise people call it by many names. So many Hindus believe the many gods are faces of one great God, the way one person can be a mom, a sister and a daughter all at once. Others feel close to one special form. Which one do you feel close to?","grownup":"Rig Veda 1.164.46 (ekam sat vipra bahudha vadanti), paraphrased. Hindus vary: some see one God in many forms, some devote themselves to one deity (Vishnu, Shiva, Devi), some speak of formless Brahman. All are Hindu answers.","source":"Rig Veda 1.164.46 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"hin-05","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why do we give food to God and then eat it?","answer":"When food is offered to God first, many Hindus believe God blesses it, and then it's called prasad, a gift that comes back to us. It's like saying thank you before we take. In the Gita, Krishna says even a leaf, a flower, a fruit or a little water, given with love, is welcome. It's the love that counts.","grownup":"Bhagavad Gita 9.26: Krishna accepts a leaf, flower, fruit or water offered with devotion. Food offered in puja (naivedya) becomes prasad, shared as grace. Gita 3.13 says those who eat what remains after offering are freed from fault.","source":"Bhagavad Gita 9.26 and 3.13 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: 'Hindus believe' softened to 'many Hindus believe'. Gita 9.26 and 3.13 confirmed."}},{"id":"hin-06","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why do we touch Dadi's feet?","answer":"Touching an elder's feet is a Hindu way of saying, I respect you and I'm grateful for you. And when Dadi touches your head back, she's giving you her blessing. It's like a hug made of respect. Some families do a namaste instead, and that's a bow from the heart too.","grownup":"Pranam or charan sparsh honors elders and teachers; the elder answers with ashirvad, a blessing. The Taittiriya Upanishad (1.11.2) teaches treating mother, father, teacher and guest as divine (matri devo bhava, pitri devo bhava…).","source":"Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"hin-07","path":"HINDUISM","q":"what is karma?","answer":"Karma means action. Many Hindus believe what we do comes back around, like a ball you bounce off a wall. Kind things grow more kindness; mean things grow more hurt. Many say it isn't God being angry; it's more like planting seeds. What's one kind seed you could plant tomorrow?","grownup":"Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5: as one acts, so one becomes. The Gita (2.47) teaches doing one's duty without clinging to results. Many Hindu teachers warn against the misreading that someone's suffering proves they were bad.","source":"Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.5; Bhagavad Gita 2.47 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Some schools (e.g., Brahma Sutra 3.2.38) hold that God dispenses karma's fruits; 'isn't God being angry' is softened with 'many Hindus say'. Fact-check 2026-10-14: 'Hindus believe' softened to 'many Hindus believe'. BU 4.4.5, Gita 2.47, Brahma Sutra 3.2.38 confirmed."}},{"id":"hin-08","path":"HINDUISM","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"Many Hindus believe the real you, the atman, never dies. The Gita says the body is like clothes: when they wear out, the soul puts on new ones. They believe the soul is born again, and one day rests in God for good. It's okay to feel sad and to miss people. What would you like to ask about it?","grownup":"Bhagavad Gita 2.20 (the self is not born and does not die) and 2.22 (worn-out clothes). Rebirth continues until moksha, liberation; schools describe moksha as oneness with Brahman or eternal closeness to God. Keep it gentle and let questions lead.","source":"Bhagavad Gita 2.20, 2.22 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: 'Hindus believe' softened to 'many Hindus believe'. Gita 2.20, 2.22 confirmed."}},{"id":"hin-09","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why is the cow special?","answer":"Many Hindus love the cow like a gentle mother, because she gives milk and asks for so little. Lord Krishna grew up as a cowherd boy who loved his cows. Being kind to the cow is a way to practice being kind to every living thing. That's why many Hindus don't eat beef.","grownup":"Reverence for the cow ties to ahimsa (non-harm) and to Krishna as Gopala, the cowherd, in the Bhagavata Purana, Book 10. Practice varies: many Hindus are vegetarian, many are not. Avoid describing it as 'worshiping animals'.","source":"Bhagavata Purana, Book 10 (paraphrase); ahimsa in Hindu ethics","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"hin-10","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why do we say om?","answer":"Om is a sound many Hindus call the sound of everything, like the hum of the whole universe. Hindus say it at the start of prayers and songs. Try it with me: take a big breath and say ommm until the air runs out. How did that feel in your chest?","grownup":"The Mandukya Upanishad opens by saying that all that is, past, present and future, is Om. It begins many mantras. Buddhists and Jains use it too, each in their own way.","source":"Mandukya Upanishad 1 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"hin-11","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why do we tie a rakhi?","answer":"On Raksha Bandhan, a sister ties a rakhi on her brother's wrist, and he promises to look out for her. Lots of families tie them on cousins and friends too, and many make the promise both ways. The thread says: we belong to each other and we'll keep each other safe. Who would you tie one for?","grownup":"Raksha Bandhan falls on the full moon of Shravan. Origin stories vary; a popular one tells of Draupadi binding Krishna's cut finger, a telling not found in the critical Mahabharata text. It's mainly a family festival, not a scriptural rite.","source":"Popular and regional tradition (no single scriptural source)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"hin-12","path":"HINDUISM","q":"why do people wear a dot on their forehead?","answer":"That dot or line is called a bindi or tilak. Many Hindus put it between the eyebrows, a spot linked with wisdom. It can mean, I've prayed today, or I'm remembering God. Different families wear different shapes and colors, and each has its own story. Want to ask Dadi what hers means?","grownup":"Marks differ by community: a U-shape for many Vaishnavas, three horizontal lines for many Shaivas. The spot between the brows is linked with the ajna chakra in yogic teaching. A married woman's red bindi or sindoor is regional custom. Meanings vary; ask family.","source":"Vaishnava and Shaiva sectarian marks; regional custom (descriptive, no single text)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-01","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"where is heaven?","answer":"Christians believe heaven is where God is, and it isn't somewhere you can fly to in a plane. Jesus told his friends that God's house has many rooms, and he would get a place ready for them. Christians believe it's full of love, with no more crying. What do you imagine it's like?","grownup":"John 14:2 (KJV: 'In my Father's house are many mansions'); Revelation 21:4 (God wipes away tears). Christians differ on details; many also hope for resurrection and a renewed creation (1 Corinthians 15). Saying 'nobody knows exactly' is honest.","source":"John 14:2 (KJV); Revelation 21:4 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-02","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"who is Jesus?","answer":"Christians believe Jesus is God's Son, who was born as a baby in Bethlehem, grew up, and showed everyone what God's love looks like. He healed people, scooped up kids in his arms, and ate with people others left out. Christians believe he died, came back to life, and is with them now. What story about him do you like?","grownup":"Matthew 2:1 (Bethlehem); Mark 10:13–16 (Jesus takes children in his arms); Luke 15:2 (eats with sinners). The Nicene Creed confesses him fully God and fully human. Jews see Jesus differently; Muslims honor him as a prophet. Say so kindly.","source":"Matthew 2:1; Mark 10:16; Luke 15:2 (paraphrase); Nicene Creed","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-03","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"why did Jesus have to die?","answer":"That's a big one, and grown-ups still wonder about it too. Christians believe Jesus loved people so much that he didn't run away, even when it cost his life. They believe his death and coming back to life opened a way for everyone to be close to God. And the story doesn't end on the cross. It ends with Easter.","grownup":"John 15:13 (KJV: 'Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends'). Churches explain the atonement in several ways. Historically, Rome executed Jesus; never let a child hear 'the Jews killed Jesus'.","source":"John 15:13 (KJV)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-04","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"what is Easter about?","answer":"Easter is one of the happiest days of the year for Christians. They believe Jesus died on a Friday, and on Sunday morning his friends found the tomb empty: he was alive again! Easter eggs came later. Some Christians say a chick hatching from an egg is like new life breaking out. Want to dye eggs and tell the story?","grownup":"The empty tomb: Mark 16, Matthew 28, Luke 24, John 20. Egg customs are cultural, not biblical, though many Christians use them as a resurrection symbol. Eastern and Western churches often celebrate on different dates.","source":"Mark 16:1–8; John 20:1–18 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Unsourced superlative ('the happiest day') softened; 'many' to 'some' for the egg symbol."}},{"id":"chr-05","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"why do we celebrate Christmas?","answer":"At Christmas, Christians celebrate Jesus being born. The Bible says he was born in a little town called Bethlehem and laid in a manger, a feeding box for animals, because there was no room anywhere else. Christians believe God came as a baby to show how close God wants to be to us.","grownup":"Luke 2:7 (KJV: 'laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn'). The Bible gives no date; December 25 is church tradition. Many Orthodox churches celebrate on January 7 by the Julian calendar.","source":"Luke 2:1–20 (KJV)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-06","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"does God hear me when I pray?","answer":"Christians believe God hears every prayer: big ones, tiny ones, even the ones you don't say out loud. Jesus taught his friends to call God Father, like talking to a parent who loves you. Christians say sometimes the answer is yes, sometimes wait, sometimes something different. Would you like to tell God something tonight?","grownup":"Matthew 6:6–9 (pray in secret; 'Our Father'); Romans 8:26 (the Spirit helps when we lack words). Be gentle about prayers that seem unanswered; many Christians speak of trust rather than guarantees.","source":"Matthew 6:6–9; Romans 8:26 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-07","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"what is the Bible?","answer":"The Bible is a whole library in one book. It has stories, songs, poems, letters and wise sayings, written by many people over a very long time. Christians believe God speaks through it. Its first part is shared with Jewish people, and the second part tells about Jesus. Want to pick a story tonight?","grownup":"Christians read the Old Testament (largely the Hebrew Bible, the Tanakh) and the New Testament. Catholic and Orthodox Bibles include books (the deuterocanon) most Protestant Bibles don't. 2 Timothy 3:16 calls scripture inspired.","source":"2 Timothy 3:16 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-08","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"why do people get baptized?","answer":"Baptism is when someone is washed with water to show they belong to God's family. Jesus was baptized in a river. Some churches baptize babies, and some wait until people can choose for themselves. Either way, Christians believe it's a fresh start with God. Want to hear the story of Jesus's baptism?","grownup":"Matthew 3:13–17 (Jesus's baptism); Matthew 28:19. Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican, Lutheran and Methodist churches baptize infants; Baptists and many evangelicals baptize believers. Name both kindly; it often matters in interfaith homes.","source":"Matthew 3:13–17; Matthew 28:19 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-09","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"what does the cross mean?","answer":"The cross reminds Christians of the day Jesus died, but they wear it as a sign of love and hope, because they believe he rose again. It's a little like a scar that tells a story: something hard happened, and love was stronger. Some crosses are empty, to remember Easter morning.","grownup":"1 Corinthians 1:18. Catholics and Orthodox Christians often use a crucifix, showing Jesus's body; many Protestant churches use an empty cross to emphasize the resurrection. Both point to the same story.","source":"1 Corinthians 1:18 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-10","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"does God love bad people?","answer":"Christians believe yes. God loves everyone, even people who do bad things, and even you on your grumpiest day. The Bible says God loved people before they ever got it right. That doesn't mean bad things are okay. It means God never stops hoping we'll come back and do better. That's a big love.","grownup":"Romans 5:8 (KJV: God's love shown 'while we were yet sinners'). Luke 15: the lost sheep, coin and son. Pair grace with repair: Matthew 5:23–24 asks people to make peace with someone they've hurt.","source":"Romans 5:8 (KJV); Luke 15; Matthew 5:23–24","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-11","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"how can God be three?","answer":"Most Christians believe there's one God who is Father, Son and Holy Spirit all at once. They call it the Trinity, and it's a mystery even grown-ups can't fully explain. Some people picture one family of love. Others say no picture quite fits. It's okay to wonder about it. What do you think it means?","grownup":"Matthew 28:19; 2 Corinthians 13:14. Defined in the Nicene Creed (325, 381). Every analogy (water, ice and steam; the shamrock) falls short, and theologians warn about several. A few groups, such as Unitarians, understand God differently.","source":"Matthew 28:19; 2 Corinthians 13:14; Nicene Creed","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"chr-12","path":"CHRISTIANITY","q":"why do we sing in church?","answer":"Christians sing because singing is a way to pray with your whole body, and the Bible is full of songs. The book of Psalms is a whole songbook! Singing together also helps everyone feel like one family. Some churches have bands, some have choirs, some just voices. Want to sing one together?","grownup":"Psalm 100:1 (KJV: 'Make a joyful noise unto the LORD'); Colossians 3:16 (psalms, hymns and spiritual songs). Christian music ranges from chant and hymns to gospel and worship bands.","source":"Psalm 100:1 (KJV); Colossians 3:16","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-01","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do we eat a wafer at Mass?","answer":"At the Last Supper, Jesus took bread and said, this is my body; do this to remember me. Catholics believe that at Mass the bread truly becomes Jesus, even though it still looks like bread. So when people receive it, they're meeting Jesus up close. That's why everyone gets so quiet and careful.","grownup":"Luke 22:19; John 6:51–58. The Church teaches the Real Presence and transubstantiation (Catechism 1374–1376). First Communion comes at the age of reason, often around seven, after preparation. Many Protestants understand communion differently.","source":"Luke 22:19 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism of the Catholic Church 1374–1376","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-02","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do we pray to Mary?","answer":"Catholics don't pray to Mary instead of God. They ask her to pray for them, the way you might ask Grandma to pray for you. She's Jesus's mother, so Catholics love her like a mother too. The Hail Mary even begins with the angel's own words to her in the Bible.","grownup":"The Hail Mary's opening joins Luke 1:28 and 1:42. The Church distinguishes honor given to Mary and the saints from worship owed to God alone (Catechism 971, 2677). Many Protestants pray to God directly and don't ask saints for prayers.","source":"Luke 1:28, 1:42 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism 971, 2677","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-03","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"who are the saints?","answer":"Saints are people who loved God and others in a big way, and Catholics believe they're now with God in heaven. Some were kings, some were kids, some cooked and cleaned. Catholics ask saints to pray for them, like friends cheering from heaven. Your name might even belong to a saint! Want to look it up?","grownup":"Canonization declares that a person is in heaven and may be publicly honored (Catechism 828). Hebrews 12:1 speaks of a 'cloud of witnesses'. Francisco and Jacinta Marto, canonized in 2017, were children. Feast days and name-saints are lovely hooks for kids.","source":"Catechism 828; Hebrews 12:1","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-04","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why does the priest wear different colors?","answer":"The colors tell you what season of the Church year it is. Purple means getting ready, in Advent and Lent. White or gold means a big celebration, like Christmas and Easter. Green is the ordinary growing time. Red is for the Holy Spirit and for saints who died for their faith. What color did Father wear today?","grownup":"The General Instruction of the Roman Missal (346) sets the colors: white, red, green and violet, with black and rose as options. Rose may be worn on Gaudete and Laetare Sundays. Colors are a great way for kids to 'read' the Mass.","source":"General Instruction of the Roman Missal 346","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-05","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do we make the sign of the cross?","answer":"When Catholics touch their forehead, chest and shoulders, they're drawing a cross on themselves and saying, in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. It's a quick way to say, I belong to God, and God is with me. Catholics do it to start and end their prayers.","grownup":"The words echo Matthew 28:19. Tertullian (On the Crown, ch. 3, around 200) describes Christians tracing the sign on their foreheads. Latin Catholics cross left to right; Eastern Christians right to left.","source":"Matthew 28:19; Tertullian, De Corona 3 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-06","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do people get ashes on their forehead?","answer":"On Ash Wednesday, the priest puts ashes on your forehead in a cross and may say, remember that you are dust, and to dust you shall return. Catholics hear it as: you are small, God is great, and God loves you anyway. It starts Lent, a time to grow closer to God and kinder to others.","grownup":"Genesis 3:19; the other formula is 'Repent, and believe in the Gospel' (Mark 1:15). The ashes are traditionally made from the previous year's Palm Sunday palms. With a worried child, talk about beginnings, not death.","source":"Genesis 3:19 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Roman Missal, Ash Wednesday","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-07","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do we go to confession?","answer":"Everybody makes mistakes, even grown-ups. In confession, Catholics tell God they're sorry, out loud, to a priest who listens for God. Then the priest says that God forgives them. It's meant to feel like putting down a heavy backpack. And the priest keeps what you say secret, always.","grownup":"The sacrament of Penance and Reconciliation (Catechism 1422–1498), rooted in John 20:22–23. The seal of confession is absolute (Catechism 1467). First confession usually comes before First Communion.","source":"John 20:22–23; Catechism 1422–1498, 1467","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-08","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do we touch holy water?","answer":"Holy water is water a priest has blessed. When Catholics dip their fingers in it at the church door and make the sign of the cross, it reminds them of their baptism, the day they joined God's family. It's like a little welcome home every time you walk in.","grownup":"Holy water is a sacramental (Catechism 1667–1679), and its use at the church door recalls baptism. Some families keep a small font at home.","source":"Catechism 1667–1679","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-09","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"do I have a guardian angel?","answer":"Catholics believe God gives every person a guardian angel, a helper who watches over them from the very beginning of their life. Jesus said that the angels of little ones always see God's face. There's even a short prayer to your angel. You can talk to yours tonight, if you'd like.","grownup":"Matthew 18:10; Catechism 336: angels watch over human life from beginning to death, and, quoting St. Basil, beside each believer stands an angel (paraphrase). The traditional 'Angel of God' prayer is often a child's first. The Guardian Angels' feast is October 2.","source":"Matthew 18:10 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism 336","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Grown-up note now reflects what CCC 336 actually says (quoting Basil: 'beside each believer'); a personal guardian angel for every person is common teaching rather than defined dogma."}},{"id":"cat-10","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why can't Father get married?","answer":"In most Catholic churches, priests promise not to marry so they can give all their love and time to God and to everyone in the parish, a bit like Jesus did. It's a rule of the Church, not a rule against love. And in some Catholic churches, the Eastern ones, priests can be married!","grownup":"Celibacy in the Latin Church is a discipline, not doctrine (Catechism 1579–1580; Canon 277). Eastern Catholic Churches ordain married men, and some married former Anglican clergy are Latin priests. The usual texts: Matthew 19:12, 1 Corinthians 7:32–33.","source":"Catechism 1579–1580; Code of Canon Law 277; Matthew 19:12","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-11","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"who is the Pope?","answer":"The Pope is the leader of the whole Catholic Church, a bit like a grandfather for Catholics all over the world. Catholics believe Jesus picked Peter to lead his friends, and the Pope takes Peter's place today. He lives at the Vatican, in Rome, and prays for the whole world, not just Catholics.","grownup":"Matthew 16:18–19 (Peter, the rock); Catechism 880–882. Orthodox and Protestant Christians understand Peter's role and the papacy differently; say so if the other side of the family is Christian too.","source":"Matthew 16:18–19 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism 880–882","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"cat-12","path":"CATHOLIC","q":"why do we pray for people who died?","answer":"Catholics believe love doesn't stop when someone dies. So they keep praying for the people they miss, asking God to bring them all the way home to heaven. On November 2nd, All Souls Day, Catholics remember everyone who has died. Who would you like to pray for, or just remember, tonight?","grownup":"2 Maccabees 12:46 (in the Catholic canon); Catechism 1030–1032 on purgatory as purification. Many Protestants don't pray for the dead; Orthodox Christians do, in their own way. Keep purgatory gentle: 'getting ready', not punishment.","source":"2 Maccabees 12:46 (Douay-Rheims numbering; 12:45-46 in many modern Bibles), paraphrase; Catechism 1030–1032","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: 'Most Protestants' softened to 'many' (some Anglicans and Lutherans do pray for the dead); verse-numbering difference noted."}},{"id":"jud-01","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why don't we have Christmas?","answer":"Christmas is the birthday of Jesus, and it's a special holiday for Christians. Jews have their own holidays from the Jewish story, like Hanukkah, Passover, and Shabbat every single week! Jewish families can still wish friends a merry Christmas and enjoy their lights. Being different from friends is okay. Which Jewish holiday do you love most?","grownup":"Christmas celebrates a Christian belief about Jesus. Hanukkah falls nearby but is a minor festival in Jewish law; its size in America is partly cultural. In interfaith homes, many families keep each holiday as its own tradition's, not merged.","source":"General; Hanukkah: Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 21b (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-02","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why do we light Shabbat candles?","answer":"On Friday night, Jewish families light candles to welcome Shabbat, the day of rest. The Torah says both remember the Sabbath and observe the Sabbath, and many say two candles stand for those two words. Lighting them is like saying: the week is done, now we rest together. Want to help light them this Friday?","grownup":"Exodus 20:8 ('Remember the sabbath day') and Deuteronomy 5:12 ('Observe the sabbath day'), JPS 1917. Linking the two candles to these two words is a well-known teaching. Candles are lit before sunset, eyes covered for the blessing.","source":"Exodus 20:8; Deuteronomy 5:12 (JPS 1917)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Answer now uses 'observe', matching JPS 1917 Deuteronomy 5:12, which the grown-up note quotes. Exodus 20:8 / Deuteronomy 5:12 wording confirmed; the two-candles link is a custom, labeled as such."}},{"id":"jud-03","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why do we eat matzah on Passover?","answer":"On Passover, Jews remember how their ancestors left slavery in Egypt. They left so fast their bread didn't have time to rise, so it came out flat: matzah. Eating it helps Jews remember being slaves and then being free, and to care about anyone who isn't free today. Want to taste some?","grownup":"Exodus 12:39: the dough wasn't leavened because they were thrust out of Egypt. The Seder retells the Exodus especially for children (Exodus 13:8), and the youngest traditionally asks the Four Questions.","source":"Exodus 12:39; Exodus 13:8 (JPS 1917, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-04","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why do people wear a kippah?","answer":"A kippah is a little cap. Many Jews wear one to remember that God is above us and with us all the time. Some wear it every day, some only when praying or at synagogue. In some communities girls and women wear one too. It's like a small hello to God, right on your head.","grownup":"Covering the head is a custom, not a Torah law; the Talmud links it to awe of heaven (Shabbat 156b, paraphrase). Practice differs across Orthodox, Conservative, Reform and Reconstructionist communities.","source":"Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 156b (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-05","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why don't we eat pork?","answer":"The Torah gives Jews food rules called kashrut, and one is not to eat pork. Jews keep these rules to make even ordinary meals a way of remembering God. Families keep kosher in different ways: some strictly, some partly. It isn't that pigs are bad animals. It's about what Jews choose to eat.","grownup":"Leviticus 11:7; Deuteronomy 14:8. Many teachers say these laws aren't about hygiene but holiness (Leviticus 11:44–45). Observance varies widely; describe your family's practice without judging others'.","source":"Leviticus 11:7, 11:44–45; Deuteronomy 14:8","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-06","path":"JUDAISM","q":"what's the little box on our door?","answer":"That's a mezuzah. Inside is a tiny scroll with the Shema, a very special Jewish prayer about loving God with all your heart. The Torah says to write these words on the doorposts of your house. Many Jews touch it when they walk by, then kiss their fingers, like a kiss hello.","grownup":"Deuteronomy 6:4–9 and 11:13–21 are written on the parchment; 6:9 commands writing them on doorposts. It's placed on the right side; the Ashkenazi slant is remembered as a compromise between Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam. Many Sephardim hang it upright.","source":"Deuteronomy 6:4–9, 11:13–21 (JPS 1917, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Slant is an Ashkenazi custom, not universal; Sephardic practice added."}},{"id":"jud-07","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why do we light eight candles on Hanukkah?","answer":"Long ago, a small group called the Maccabees won back the Temple in Jerusalem. The story says they found only enough oil for one day, but the lamp burned for eight! So Jews light one more candle each night. It's about light winning, and being free to be Jewish. Want to count them together?","grownup":"The oil story is in the Talmud (Shabbat 21b). The books of Maccabees (1 Maccabees 4) tell of the rededication without the oil miracle; they're in Catholic and Orthodox Bibles, not the Jewish canon.","source":"Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 21b (paraphrase); 1 Maccabees 4:36–59","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-08","path":"JUDAISM","q":"what's a bar or bat mitzvah?","answer":"When a Jewish boy turns 13, or a girl turns 12 or 13, they become a bar or bat mitzvah: a son or daughter of the commandments. They're old enough to take on Jewish responsibilities. Many read from the Torah in front of everyone, and then there's a party! What would you be proud to learn?","grownup":"The age comes from rabbinic tradition (Mishnah Avot 5:21: at thirteen, for the commandments). Bat mitzvah ceremonies became common in the 20th century; practice differs across movements. The status comes with the age; the ceremony celebrates it.","source":"Mishnah Avot 5:21 (paraphrase; numbering varies by edition)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: 'began' softened to 'became common' (dating the first ceremony is debated). Mishnah Avot 5:21 is the Sefaria/standard numbering; kept the 'numbering varies' flag."}},{"id":"jud-09","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why do they blow the shofar?","answer":"The shofar is a ram's horn. On Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year, it's blown like a wake-up alarm for the heart. It says: think about this year, say sorry where you need to, and start fresh. It's blown again at the very end of Yom Kippur. Can you make the long blast sound?","grownup":"Leviticus 23:24 and Numbers 29:1 speak of a day of blowing the horn. Maimonides reads the shofar as a call to wake up and return (Mishneh Torah, Laws of Repentance 3:4, paraphrase).","source":"Leviticus 23:24; Numbers 29:1; Maimonides, Hilchot Teshuvah 3:4 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-10","path":"JUDAISM","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"Jewish people have many ideas about what happens after we die, and they've wondered about it for a very long time. Many believe the soul goes on with God. Jews also do something together: they keep the person's memory alive. They say Kaddish, light a candle, and tell stories. They say, may their memory be a blessing.","grownup":"Judaism emphasizes this life. Afterlife teaching ranges from the world to come and resurrection (Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1) to more open views. Mourning: shiva, Kaddish, yahrzeit. 'Zichrono livracha', may his memory be a blessing, is the familiar phrase.","source":"Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-11","path":"JUDAISM","q":"what is the Shema?","answer":"The Shema is a very special Jewish prayer. It begins: Hear, O Israel, the Lord our God, the Lord is one. Many Jews say it in the morning, at night, and right before sleep. Some cover their eyes while they say it, to really listen. Want to say it at bedtime together?","grownup":"Deuteronomy 6:4 (JPS 1917: 'Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one.'). Said morning and evening (Mishnah Berakhot 1) and at bedtime. Covering the eyes follows Rabbi Judah the Prince (Berakhot 13b).","source":"Deuteronomy 6:4 (JPS 1917); Mishnah Berakhot 1; Babylonian Talmud Berakhot 13b","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"jud-12","path":"JUDAISM","q":"why do we say sorry on Yom Kippur?","answer":"On Yom Kippur, Jews ask God to forgive them. But there's a rule: if you hurt a person, you have to say sorry to that person first. God doesn't do that part for you. So before Yom Kippur, many people call friends and family to make things right. Is there anyone you'd like to make up with?","grownup":"Mishnah Yoma 8:9: Yom Kippur atones for wrongs between a person and God; for wrongs between people, only once the person is appeased. Yom Kippur is a fast day (Leviticus 16:29–31), with prayer from evening to evening.","source":"Mishnah Yoma 8:9 (paraphrase); Leviticus 16:29–31","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-01","path":"ISLAM","q":"why do we pray five times a day?","answer":"Muslims pray five times a day: at dawn, midday, afternoon, sunset and night. It's like checking in with Allah all day long, so you never go too long without remembering. Muslims say the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ received this gift of prayer on a special night journey. Want to watch the sky change with me at sunset?","grownup":"The Qur'an commands prayer at fixed hours (4:103) and names times of day (11:114, 17:78). The number five comes from hadith about the Night Journey (Sahih al-Bukhari 349, sunnah.com numbering). Many Shia Muslims combine the five into three sessions.","source":"Qur'an 4:103, 11:114, 17:78 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase); Sahih al-Bukhari 349, sunnah.com numbering (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Confirm Bukhari 349 numbering for the mi'raj hadith in the standard (USC-MSA vs. Fath al-Bari) numbering. Fact-check 2026-10-14: Bukhari 349 confirmed: the opening hadith of the Book of Prayer (Abu Dharr's account of the Night Journey and the fifty-to-five prayers), sunnah.com numbering."}},{"id":"isl-02","path":"ISLAM","q":"why do we fast in Ramadan?","answer":"In Ramadan, grown-up Muslims don't eat or drink from dawn until sunset, for a whole month. It helps them remember Allah, feel thankful, and understand how hungry people feel. Kids often practice with a half-day first. At sunset everyone breaks the fast together, often with a date. Want to help set the iftar table?","grownup":"Qur'an 2:183–185 prescribes the fast so believers may become mindful of God. The sick and travelers are exempt (2:184); children aren't required to fast before puberty. Breaking the fast with dates follows the Prophet's practice (reported in Sunan Abu Dawud).","source":"Qur'an 2:183–185 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase); Sunan Abu Dawud, Book of Fasting (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-03","path":"ISLAM","q":"why don't we have pictures of the Prophet?","answer":"Muslims love the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ dearly, and Muslims traditionally don't make pictures of him. They want people to remember his kindness and his words, not a picture someone made up. It also keeps the focus on Allah, never on statues or images. So Muslims tell stories about him instead. Want to hear one?","grownup":"This rests mainly on hadith against image-making and guarding against idolatry, not a Qur'an verse. Sunni practice broadly avoids depicting him; some Persian and Ottoman paintings did, often veiling his face, and images appear in some Shia settings. Treat it as deeply felt love and respect.","source":"Hadith on image-making (e.g., Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Dress), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Sweeping 'Muslims don't draw his face' softened to 'traditionally'; grown-up note now names Shia practice fairly. No graphic hadith content quoted."}},{"id":"isl-04","path":"ISLAM","q":"why do we say bismillah?","answer":"Bismillah means in the name of Allah. Muslims say it before eating, starting homework or getting in the car, as a way of saying: Allah, please be with me in this. Almost every chapter of the Qur'an begins with it. What's something you'd like to start with bismillah tomorrow?","grownup":"The full form opens the Qur'an (1:1, Pickthall 1930: 'In the name of Allah, the Beneficent, the Merciful') and every surah except the ninth. Saying it before meals is taught in hadith (Sahih al-Bukhari 5376).","source":"Qur'an 1:1 (Pickthall 1930); Sahih al-Bukhari 5376 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-05","path":"ISLAM","q":"why does Mama wear a hijab?","answer":"Many Muslim women wear a hijab to cover their hair because they believe Allah asks them to dress modestly. Some women say it feels like a crown, a way of saying, I belong to Allah. Muslim women wear it in many styles, and some don't wear one. What do you like about Mama's?","grownup":"Qur'an 24:31 and 33:59 address modest dress; most classical jurists read them as requiring a head covering, while some modern scholars differ. Practice varies by culture and person. Avoid framing it as imposed, or as a measure of devotion.","source":"Qur'an 24:31, 33:59 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Unsourced 'for lots of women it feels' softened to 'some women say'; tone keeps it a choice of faith, notes that some don't wear one."}},{"id":"isl-06","path":"ISLAM","q":"why don't we eat pork?","answer":"The Qur'an tells Muslims not to eat pork. Muslims follow it because they trust that Allah knows what's best, the way you trust a parent's rule even when you don't know every reason. Food that's allowed is called halal. Lots of Jewish families don't eat pork either!","grownup":"Qur'an 2:173, 5:3, 6:145 and 16:115 forbid swine; necessity lifts the rule (2:173). Halal also covers how animals are slaughtered and avoiding intoxicants (5:90). Kosher and halal overlap but aren't identical.","source":"Qur'an 2:173, 5:3, 6:145, 16:115 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-07","path":"ISLAM","q":"what is Eid?","answer":"Muslims have two big Eids. Eid al-Fitr is the party after Ramadan: new clothes, prayers, sweets and gifts. Eid al-Adha remembers how Prophet Ibrahim trusted Allah, and families share food with neighbors and people in need. Both start with a special prayer and lots of hugs. Eid Mubarak means blessed Eid!","grownup":"Eid al-Fitr ends Ramadan; zakat al-fitr is given before its prayer. Eid al-Adha comes with the Hajj and recalls Ibrahim's readiness to sacrifice his son, who was spared (Qur'an 37:102–107). Keep that detail for older kids.","source":"Qur'an 37:102–107 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-08","path":"ISLAM","q":"why do we face Mecca when we pray?","answer":"Muslims all over the world face the Kaaba in Mecca when they pray. Muslims believe Prophet Ibrahim and his son built it as a house for worshiping one God. Imagine everyone, in every country, turning toward the same place, like spokes on a bicycle wheel. Muslims don't pray to the Kaaba; they pray to Allah, together.","grownup":"Qur'an 2:144 sets the prayer direction (qibla) toward the Sacred Mosque; 2:127 tells of Ibrahim and Ismail raising the House's foundations. Muslims are clear that the Kaaba itself is not worshiped.","source":"Qur'an 2:127, 2:144 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-09","path":"ISLAM","q":"who is Allah?","answer":"Allah is the Arabic word for God. Arab Christians say Allah too! Muslims believe Allah is one, made everything, and is more merciful than anyone. The Qur'an teaches Muslims to tell Jews and Christians: our God and your God is one, though each understands God in their own way. What's a question you'd like to ask about God?","grownup":"Qur'an 29:46 (Pickthall: 'our God and your God is One'), words Muslims are told to say to the People of the Scripture. Arabic-speaking Christians use 'Allah' in their Bibles. Real differences remain, for example on the Trinity (112:1–4); name them honestly.","source":"Qur'an 29:46 (Pickthall 1930); Qur'an 112","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Fixed attribution: in 29:46 Muslims are told to say this to the People of the Scripture; the Qur'an is not addressing Jews and Christians directly. Pickthall wording confirmed."}},{"id":"isl-10","path":"ISLAM","q":"what is the Qur'an?","answer":"The Qur'an is the holy book of Islam. Muslims believe Allah's words came to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ through the angel Jibril, a little at a time, over many years. It's in Arabic and meant to be recited out loud, so it sounds beautiful. Many kids learn parts of it by heart. Shall we listen to a recitation?","grownup":"Qur'an 96:1–5 is traditionally the first revelation ('Read!'). Tradition says revelation spanned about twenty-three years. Muslims see translations as interpretations; Pickthall titled his The Meaning of the Glorious Koran for that reason.","source":"Qur'an 96:1–5 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-11","path":"ISLAM","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"Muslims believe that when someone dies, their soul goes back to Allah, who made them and loves them. Muslims say a verse that means: we belong to Allah, and to Him we return. They hope for Jannah, a beautiful garden. And they keep praying for the people they miss. Who would you like to make dua for?","grownup":"Qur'an 2:156 (Pickthall 1930: 'Lo! we are Allah's and lo! unto Him we are returning'). Paradise is described as gardens (e.g., 13:35). Dua and charity for the dead are encouraged. Leave talk of the grave's trials for older children.","source":"Qur'an 2:156 (Pickthall 1930); Qur'an 13:35","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"isl-12","path":"ISLAM","q":"why do we say assalamu alaikum?","answer":"Assalamu alaikum means peace be upon you. It's how Muslims say hello, and the answer is wa alaikum assalam: and upon you be peace. So every hello is a little wish for peace! The Qur'an says when someone greets you, greet them back with something even better. Want to try it?","grownup":"Qur'an 4:86: return a greeting with a better one, or its like. A hadith teaches that spreading salaam builds love among people (Sahih Muslim 54). Hebrew 'shalom aleichem' is a close cousin of the greeting.","source":"Qur'an 4:86 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase); Sahih Muslim 54 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-01","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why does Papa wear a turban?","answer":"Papa's turban is called a dastar. It's a way of saying, I'm a Sikh and I'm proud of it. It keeps his uncut hair neat and covered. The Gurus wore turbans, and in their time a turban was a sign of honor, so Sikhs say it shows everyone is equal and noble. It's like a crown from the Guru.","grownup":"The dastar covers kesh, uncut hair, one of the Five Ks. The Sikh Rehat Maryada requires it of initiated (Amritdhari) men; many women wear one too. It stands for dignity, equality and commitment. Boys often start with a patka.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-02","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why don't Sikhs cut their hair?","answer":"Sikhs believe hair is a gift from Waheguru, part of how God made us, so they keep it uncut and take good care of it. Combing it every day with a little comb called a kanga is part of looking after the gift. Uncut hair is called kesh, one of the Five Ks. Want to learn the other four?","grownup":"The Five Ks: kesh (uncut hair), kanga (comb), kara (steel bracelet), kachera (undershorts), kirpan (sword). Required for Amritdhari Sikhs; many others keep some or all. Tradition links them to the Khalsa's founding at Vaisakhi, 1699.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-03","path":"SIKHISM","q":"what is the bracelet Papa wears?","answer":"It's called a kara, a steel bracelet. It's a circle with no beginning and no end, and Sikhs say that's like Waheguru. When Sikhs see it on their wrist, it reminds them: do good things with these hands. It's like tying a string on your finger to remember something important.","grownup":"One of the Five Ks. Plain steel rather than gold signals simplicity and strength. Its meanings (God's infinity, the bond with the Guru, restraint of the hands) are traditional teaching; the Rehat Maryada lists it without explanation.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada; traditional teaching (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-04","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why does everyone eat on the floor at the gurdwara?","answer":"That meal is called langar, a free kitchen. Anyone can come and eat, rich or poor, Sikh or not, and everyone sits together on the floor so nobody is above anybody else. Sikhs say Guru Nanak started it because we're all one family. Volunteers cook and serve. Want to help make rotis next time?","grownup":"Tradition traces langar to Guru Nanak; Guru Amar Das asked visitors to eat in the langar before meeting him. Sitting in rows (pangat) erases caste and status. Food is vegetarian so anyone can eat it.","source":"Sikh historical tradition; Sikh Rehat Maryada on langar (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-05","path":"SIKHISM","q":"who was Guru Nanak?","answer":"Guru Nanak was the first Sikh Guru, born in Punjab long ago. Sikhs tell that after a special experience by a river, he said there is no Hindu and no Muslim, meaning we're all children of one God. He traveled far, sang songs about God, and taught people to work honestly, share, and remember God.","grownup":"Born 1469 at Talwandi, now Nankana Sahib in Pakistan. The janamsakhis, traditional life stories, tell of his three days in the river and his words 'na koi Hindu, na koi Musalman'. Core teaching: naam japna, kirat karni, vand chhakna.","source":"Janamsakhi tradition (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-06","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why do we cover our heads in the gurdwara?","answer":"In the gurdwara, everyone covers their head and takes off their shoes, to show respect for the Guru Granth Sahib, the holy book. It's like how you'd stand up straight when someone you love and respect walks in. Visitors of any religion are welcome, and they can borrow a scarf at the door.","grownup":"The Sikh Rehat Maryada asks for covered heads and bare feet in the presence of the Guru Granth Sahib; tobacco and alcohol aren't allowed. Visitors are welcome, and head scarves are usually available.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-07","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why is a book called the Guru?","answer":"The Guru Granth Sahib is the Sikhs' holy book, and Sikhs treat it as their living Guru, their teacher. The tenth Guru, Guru Gobind Singh, said that after him the Guru would be the scripture. It's full of songs about God, by the Gurus and even by saints from Hindu and Muslim backgrounds. That's why people bow to it.","grownup":"Guru Gobind Singh conferred Guruship on the scripture in 1708. It holds hymns of six Gurus and of bhagats such as Kabir, Namdev, Ravidas and Sheikh Farid, arranged by raag (musical mode); the standard edition has 1,430 Angs (pages).","source":"Sikh historical tradition; Guru Granth Sahib (standard 1,430-Ang edition)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Bhagats such as Kabir rejected both labels; reworded to 'from Hindu and Muslim backgrounds'."}},{"id":"sik-08","path":"SIKHISM","q":"what does Waheguru mean?","answer":"Waheguru is a Sikh name for God. It's a little like saying: wow, wonderful teacher! Sikhs say it when they're amazed, thankful, sad, or praying. Some Sikhs say it over and over, quietly, to feel close to God. Want to try saying it slowly, three times, with me?","grownup":"'Vah' is an exclamation of wonder; 'guru' means teacher. Naam simran, remembering God by repeating the Name, is central practice. The word appears in the Bhatt swayyas near the end of the Guru Granth Sahib.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Bhatt Gayand's swayyas, Ang 1402-1404 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Confirm the Ang reference for 'Vahiguru' in the swayyas of Bhatt Gayand. Fact-check 2026-10-14: Ang confirmed via BaniDB: 'vaahiguroo vaahiguroo vaahiguroo vaeh jeeau' is on Ang 1402 and recurs on 1403-1404 in Bhatt Gayand's swayyas. Removed the popular 'dispels darkness' etymology (preacher's etymology)."}},{"id":"sik-09","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why are Sikhs named Singh and Kaur?","answer":"Long ago, Guru Gobind Singh gave Sikh men the name Singh, which means lion, and Sikh women the name Kaur, often translated as princess. Back then, many last names showed if your family was rich or poor, high or low. Giving everyone the same names said: we're all equal, and we're all brave.","grownup":"Tradition links the names to the founding of the Khalsa at Vaisakhi, 1699. Caste-based surnames were common; shared names rejected caste. 'Kaur' is often glossed as princess, though its precise origin is debated.","source":"Sikh historical tradition; Sikh Rehat Maryada (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-10","path":"SIKHISM","q":"what is Ik Onkar?","answer":"Ik Onkar is the very first thing written in the Sikh holy book. Ik means one. Sikhs say it means there is one God, who is in everything and everyone. That's why Sikhs believe all people are equal: the same light is inside each of us. You'll see the symbol at the gurdwara. Want to draw it?","grownup":"Ik Onkar opens the Mool Mantar on Ang 1 of the Guru Granth Sahib, which goes on to describe God as true, creator, without fear and without hate (paraphrase). The teaching that one light shines in all (e.g., Ang 13) grounds Sikh equality.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 1 (Mool Mantar) and Ang 13, paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-11","path":"SIKHISM","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"Sikhs believe the soul comes from Waheguru and, after a life of loving God and helping others, goes back home to Waheguru, like a drop of water returning to the ocean. Sikhs see death as part of God's will, so they sing hymns together. It's still okay to miss someone and to cry. What would you like to ask?","grownup":"Sikh teaching speaks of the soul's cycle of births ending in merging with God through the Name and grace. At funerals, Kirtan Sohila and Ardas are recited; the Rehat Maryada discourages loud wailing. The drop-and-ocean image recurs in Gurbani.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (funeral ceremonies); Gurbani imagery (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"sik-12","path":"SIKHISM","q":"why do some Sikhs carry a small sword?","answer":"It's called a kirpan, one of the Five Ks. It isn't for fighting or scaring anyone. It's a promise: I will stand up for people who are being hurt, and I'll never be a bully. Sikhs say it's about courage and kindness together, and they keep it small and safely tucked away.","grownup":"Carried by Amritdhari Sikhs; a popular explanation links the word to kirpa (mercy) and aan (honor). Its duty is defending the weak and justice. Guru Gobind Singh's Zafarnama allows the sword only when all other means have failed (paraphrase).","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada; Zafarnama of Guru Gobind Singh (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: kirpa + aan is a folk etymology; labeled as a popular explanation."}},{"id":"bud-01","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"is the Buddha God?","answer":"Buddhists don't believe the Buddha is God. He was a man, a prince named Siddhartha, who found a way to be free from unhappiness and taught it to everyone. Buddha means the one who woke up. Buddhists honor him like a great, loving teacher. Who's a teacher you really look up to?","grownup":"In the Dona Sutta (Anguttara Nikaya 4.36), asked whether he is a god, the Buddha says he is awakened. Mahayana traditions see buddhas more cosmically. Buddhism is generally nontheistic, though gods (devas) appear in its texts.","source":"Dona Sutta, Anguttara Nikaya 4.36 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-02","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why do we bow to the Buddha statue?","answer":"Buddhists bow to the Buddha statue to say thank you, the way you might bow to a teacher you love. It isn't praying to a statue. Bowing also helps them feel humble and calm. Many Buddhists bow three times: for the Buddha, for his teaching, and for the community who practice together.","grownup":"The three bows honor the Three Jewels: Buddha, Dhamma, Sangha. Images are aids to recollection; in some Mahayana and Vajrayana settings devotion is also directed to buddhas and bodhisattvas. Going for refuge (Khuddakapatha 1) opens many gatherings.","source":"Khuddakapatha 1 (the three refuges), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-03","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why does the Buddha sit with his eyes closed?","answer":"In many statues the Buddha is meditating. Meditation is like giving your mind a bath: you sit still, breathe, and watch your thoughts float by like clouds. Buddhists say this is how the Buddha woke up, sitting quietly under a tree. Want to try three slow breaths with me?","grownup":"Tradition places the awakening under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya. The Anapanasati Sutta (Majjhima Nikaya 118) teaches mindfulness of breathing. Many statues show half-closed eyes; the hand positions (mudras) carry meanings too.","source":"Anapanasati Sutta, Majjhima Nikaya 118 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-04","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"what is nirvana?","answer":"Nirvana is a big, peaceful freedom. Buddhists say it's like a fire going out: the fire of being greedy, angry and mixed up just stops, and what's left is calm and kind. Buddhists believe the Buddha found it, and anyone can, with practice. When do you feel most calm?","grownup":"Nibbana means extinguishing, as of a flame (Aggi-Vacchagotta Sutta, Majjhima Nikaya 72). It is the end of greed, hatred and delusion (Samyutta Nikaya 38.1). Schools describe it differently; most teachers say it is not annihilation.","source":"Majjhima Nikaya 72; Samyutta Nikaya 38.1 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-05","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why do monks shave their heads?","answer":"Buddhist monks and nuns shave their heads and wear simple robes so they don't have to think about hair or fancy clothes. That leaves more time for meditating, learning and helping people. They own very little and live on what people share with them. It's a way of saying: I'm traveling light.","grownup":"Head-shaving and robes are part of ordination under the Vinaya, the monastic rule. Robe colors differ: ochre or saffron in Theravada, maroon in Tibet, gray or black in much of East Asia. Monastics depend on lay offerings.","source":"Vinaya (monastic discipline), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-06","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why can't we squish bugs?","answer":"Buddhists try not to hurt any living thing, even a tiny bug, because every creature wants to live and be happy, just like you do. So instead of squishing a spider, you might carry it outside on a piece of paper. The Buddha taught kindness to every living being. Want to be our family's spider rescuer?","grownup":"The first of the Five Precepts is to abstain from killing living beings. Dhammapada 129–130 grounds it in empathy: all fear pain and love life, so compare others with yourself and do not kill (Muller 1881, paraphrase). Diet practice varies by tradition.","source":"Five Precepts; Dhammapada 129–130 (Muller 1881, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-07","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"what is karma?","answer":"Buddhists say karma is about intention, the reason behind what you do. Kind thoughts and actions are like good seeds: they grow happy fruit. Unkind ones grow unhappy fruit. Buddhists say nobody is keeping score; it's just how things grow. And you can plant a new seed any time. What's one you'll plant today?","grownup":"The Buddha defines kamma as intention (Anguttara Nikaya 6.63). Dhammapada 1–2: suffering follows an evil thought like the wheel follows the ox; happiness follows a pure one like a shadow (Muller 1881, paraphrase). Buddhist and Hindu views of karma differ.","source":"Anguttara Nikaya 6.63; Dhammapada 1–2 (Muller 1881, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-08","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why do we give food to the monks?","answer":"Giving food to monks is called dana, which means generosity. In some countries, monks walk with a bowl in the morning, and people put food in it. The monks don't ask for anything. Buddhists say giving makes the giver's heart big and happy, and the monks give back by teaching. Want to help me make something to share?","grownup":"The alms round continues in Theravada countries. Giving is the first of three grounds for making merit (Itivuttaka 60). In return, monastics give the Dhamma; Dhammapada 354 calls the gift of the teaching greater than all gifts.","source":"Itivuttaka 60; Dhammapada 354 (Muller 1881, paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Alms round is mainly Theravada; scoped. Itivuttaka 60 (three grounds of merit: giving, virtue, meditation) and Dhammapada 354 (the gift of Dhamma excels all gifts) confirmed."}},{"id":"bud-09","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"Many Buddhists believe life goes on into a new life, like one candle lighting another. The new flame isn't exactly the same, but it isn't completely different either. Buddhists remember people they miss by doing kind things in their name. It's okay to feel sad. What kind thing could we do for someone we miss?","grownup":"Rebirth without a fixed self is illustrated by a flame passed between lamps (Milindapanha, Rhys Davids translation). Merit is dedicated to the departed (Khuddakapatha 7). Pure Land Buddhists hope for rebirth in Amitabha's Pure Land.","source":"Milindapanha (Rhys Davids 1890), paraphrase; Tirokudda Kanda, Khuddakapatha 7","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-10","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why is the Buddha sometimes fat and laughing?","answer":"Good eye! The happy, round, laughing one usually isn't Siddhartha, the Buddha. He's a cheerful monk from Chinese stories called Budai, who carried a sack of gifts for children. People see him as a picture of joy and generosity. The Buddha who meditated under the tree is usually shown calm and slender.","grownup":"Budai (Hotei in Japan) was a Chinese monk, later identified in Chinese Buddhism with Maitreya, the future buddha. He is a folk and Chan figure, not the historical Siddhartha Gautama.","source":"Chinese Chan and folk tradition (descriptive)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-11","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"what is Vesak?","answer":"Vesak is a Buddhist holiday on a full moon in spring. Many Buddhists remember the Buddha's birth, his waking up, and his passing away, all on that day. People light lanterns, give food and gifts, and visit temples. It's a day to be extra kind. Want to make a paper lantern?","grownup":"Theravada tradition marks birth, awakening and final passing on the Vesakha full moon. In Japan, the Buddha's birthday (Hanamatsuri) is April 8. Some communities release captive birds, a custom now often discouraged for animal welfare.","source":"Theravada and East Asian festival tradition (descriptive)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"bud-12","path":"BUDDHISM","q":"why did the Buddha leave his palace?","answer":"The story says Prince Siddhartha grew up in a palace with everything he wanted. One day he saw people who were sick and old, and saw that everyone dies someday. He felt so sad for them that he wanted to find a way to help everyone stop hurting. So he left, to search. What would you want to help fix?","grownup":"The four sights (old person, sick person, corpse, wandering monk) are told of Siddhartha in later biographies and of a past buddha in the Mahapadana Sutta (Digha Nikaya 14). The Buddha's own briefer account is the Sukhamala Sutta (Anguttara Nikaya 3.38 in the PTS edition, 3.39 in others).","source":"Digha Nikaya 14; Anguttara Nikaya 3.38-39, Sukhamala Sutta (numbering varies by edition), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: AN 3.38 numbering: PTS splits the Sukhamala Sutta into 3.38-39; Asian editions, Sujato and Thanissaro number it 3.39. Both given."}},{"id":"spi-01","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"That's one of the biggest questions there is, and honestly, nobody knows for sure. Some people believe we go to God, some believe we come back in a new life, and some believe we rest, like a long sleep. In our family we're sure of one thing: the love we share stays with us. What do you think happens?","grownup":"This path names each tradition's answer instead of blending them. Marcus Aurelius likens a life's end to a ripe olive falling, blessing the tree that bore it (Meditations 4.48, Long 1862, paraphrase). Let your child's guess stand; 'I wonder too' is enough.","source":"Marcus Aurelius, Meditations 4.48 (Long 1862), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-02","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"do we believe in God?","answer":"People in our family might answer that differently, and that's okay. Some people feel sure God is real, some aren't sure, and some feel something bigger, like love or nature, without calling it God. You get to wonder about it your whole life, and you can change your mind. What do you think?","grownup":"This path welcomes believers, doubters and the spiritual-but-not-religious. William James (The Varieties of Religious Experience, 1902) studied religion as lived experience rather than doctrine. Model curiosity, and avoid handing down certainty either way.","source":"William James, The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-03","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"why don't we go to church or temple?","answer":"Lots of families go to a church, temple, mosque, synagogue or gurdwara, and those are beautiful places. Our family finds wonder in other ways too: outside in nature, in kindness, in quiet time, and in learning from many traditions. We can visit those places to learn, and you can always ask to go.","grownup":"Emerson's Nature (1836) and Thoreau's Walden (1854) describe meeting the sacred outdoors. Visiting houses of worship respectfully (call ahead, dress modestly, follow the hosts) is a lovely family practice in itself.","source":"Emerson, Nature (1836); Thoreau, Walden (1854), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-04","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"what do we believe, then?","answer":"We believe some really big things: that every person matters, that kindness is powerful, that the world is full of wonder, and that we can learn good ideas from lots of traditions. We don't have one holy book, so we keep looking and asking. What's something you believe is really true?","grownup":"Name your family's values out loud; kids need a 'we' too. Good anchors: the golden rule, and Epictetus's first lesson that some things are up to us and some aren't (Enchiridion 1, Long 1877, paraphrase).","source":"Epictetus, Enchiridion 1 (Long 1877), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-05","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"is it okay to not know?","answer":"Yes! Not knowing is where the best questions start. Even very wise people say, I'm not sure. A teacher in old China named Confucius said that knowing what you don't know is a kind of wisdom. So wondering isn't failing. It's exploring. What's something you wonder about?","grownup":"Analects 2.17 (Legge 1861): when you don't know a thing, allow that you don't know it; this is knowledge (paraphrase). Socrates says something similar in Plato's Apology (21d). 'I don't know, let's find out' teaches more than a tidy answer.","source":"Confucius, Analects 2.17 (Legge 1861), paraphrase; Plato, Apology 21d","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-06","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"why do we have quiet time?","answer":"Our minds are like a jar of muddy water. When we keep shaking it, everything's cloudy. When we sit still for a little while, the mud settles and things look clear again. Quiet time helps us feel calm and notice what's going on inside. Want to try one minute of quiet with me?","grownup":"The muddy-water image comes from Tao Te Ching 15: let muddy water be still and it gradually clears (Legge 1891, paraphrase). Breathing practices appear in many traditions; name them when you borrow (Buddhist anapanasati, Hindu pranayama, Christian contemplative prayer).","source":"Tao Te Ching 15 (Legge 1891), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-07","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"why am I here?","answer":"People have asked that for a very long time! Different traditions give different answers: to love God, to grow wise, to help others, to be happy. Many people say we each get to discover our own reason, and it can be as simple as being kind and loving the people around us. What do you think yours might be?","grownup":"Offer several answers, each with its owner: the Westminster Shorter Catechism (1647: 'to glorify God, and to enjoy him for ever'), Aristotle's flourishing, the Buddhist end of suffering. Then let your child add their own answer.","source":"Westminster Shorter Catechism, Q1 (1647); Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics, Book 1 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Removed the Frankl line (modern copyrighted author, loosely paraphrased, not in sources). Westminster Shorter Catechism Q1 wording ('to glorify God, and to enjoy him for ever') confirmed."}},{"id":"spi-08","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"can I pray if we're not in a religion?","answer":"Yes. Anyone can pray, or talk to whatever feels big and loving to them. You don't need special words or a special building. Some people say thank you, some ask for help, some just sit quietly and listen. Want to share what you're thankful for tonight, in your own words?","grownup":"William James calls prayer, in its wide sense, the very soul and essence of religion (Varieties, 1902, Lecture 19, paraphrase). If a child wants to pray in a grandparent's tradition, that's a lovely bridge; let the grandparent teach a real prayer of theirs.","source":"William James, The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902), Lecture 19 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: The 'soul and essence' line is James's own (Lecture 19), not a Sabatier quotation; attribution fixed."}},{"id":"spi-09","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"why do we celebrate other religions' holidays?","answer":"Holidays are like gifts from different families of the world. When we light Diwali lamps or sing at Christmas, we're learning what those days mean to the people who love them, and sharing their joy. We try to learn the real story behind each one, so we honor it and don't just borrow the fun.","grownup":"Respectful sharing means learning a holiday's meaning from people who keep it, naming whose it is, and not turning sacred rites into decor. Invite friends to explain their holiday; that's 'more alike than we think' in practice.","source":"General guidance (no single text)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-10","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"what is a soul?","answer":"Soul is a word many people use for the deepest part of you: the part that loves, wonders and feels. Many religions believe in a soul, and each describes it a bit differently. Some people aren't sure there is one. In our family we're sure of this: there's something wonderful about being you. Where do you think yours lives?","grownup":"Traditions differ: atman (Hindu), neshamah (Jewish), ruh (Islamic), the immortal soul (Christian), and anatta, no fixed self (Buddhist). Name the differences rather than blending them. Plato's Phaedo is an early philosophical case for the soul.","source":"Plato, Phaedo; comparative description (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-11","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"why should I be kind?","answer":"Because you know how it feels when someone is mean to you, and how it feels when someone is kind. Long ago, a teacher named Confucius said: don't do to others what you wouldn't want done to you. Many religions have a saying like that. Kindness makes the world better, one person at a time.","grownup":"Analects 15.23 (Legge 1861): asked for one word to live by, Confucius named reciprocity (paraphrase). Versions appear in Leviticus 19:18, Matthew 7:12, the hadith (Sahih al-Bukhari 13) and the Mahabharata. Kids learn empathy best through 'how would you feel?'","source":"Confucius, Analects 15.23 (Legge 1861), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"spi-12","path":"SPIRITUAL","q":"who do we say thank you to?","answer":"Saying thank you helps us notice the good things around us, like sunshine, a friend, or a yummy dinner. Even when we're not sure who to thank, feeling grateful makes our hearts bigger. A Roman emperor, Marcus Aurelius, once wrote a whole list of people he was thankful for. Want to make our own list?","grownup":"Book 1 of Marcus Aurelius's Meditations (Long 1862) thanks, one by one, the people who shaped him. A nightly 'three good things' habit is easy for families. Gratitude shows up in all eight paths here, which makes it a natural bridge.","source":"Marcus Aurelius, Meditations, Book 1 (Long 1862), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"int-01","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"are we Hindu or Catholic?","answer":"Our family has both! One of us grew up Hindu and one of us grew up Catholic, and both are part of your story, like knowing two languages or two grandmas' recipes. You get to learn about both, with lots of love from each side. What do you love most from each one?","grownup":"Swap in your own two traditions. Family educators suggest agreeing on your answer as a couple first: one faith, both, or one primary with respect for the other. Susan Katz Miller's Being Both (2013) describes families raising children in two faiths.","source":"Interfaith family guidance, e.g., Susan Katz Miller, Being Both (2013), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"int-02","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"which one is right?","answer":"That's a really big question, and people have asked it for ages. Each religion has things it believes are true, and the people who love them are sincere. In our family, we learn about both with respect and never make fun of what someone believes. What do you think so far?","grownup":"Children often ask this to test loyalty, not doctrine; never make them pick a parent. Both traditions hold real truth claims. Many texts speak respectfully of other paths, e.g., Rig Veda 1.164.46 and the Catholic Nostra Aetate 2 (the Church rejects nothing true and holy in other religions).","source":"Rig Veda 1.164.46 (paraphrase); Second Vatican Council, Nostra Aetate 2 (1965), paraphrase","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"int-03","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"why does Nani pray differently than Abuela?","answer":"Nani and Abuela grew up in different families, in different places, and each learned to talk to God in her own way. Nani might light a diya, and Abuela might pray the rosary. Different words, and both full of love. And they both pray for you! Want to ask each of them to show you how?","grownup":"Rituals carry family memory as well as belief. Inviting each grandparent to teach the child honors both and often softens tension between generations. The diya in aarti and the rosary are each, for someone, 'how my mother prayed'.","source":"General guidance (no single text)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"int-04","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"do they believe in the same God?","answer":"That's a question even grown-up teachers discuss kindly. Some say yes, it's one God with different names. Some say each religion understands God in a really different way. Both sides of your family pray with their whole hearts. What do you think? You can ask your grandparents too.","grownup":"It depends on the pair. Jews, Christians and Muslims trace faith to Abraham's God (Genesis 12; Qur'an 29:46); much Hindu teaching sees one reality with many names (Rig Veda 1.164.46); Buddhism is largely nontheistic. Honest difference is fine; don't force sameness.","source":"Qur'an 29:46 (Pickthall 1930); Rig Veda 1.164.46 (paraphrase)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Added a Jewish/Christian source so the Abrahamic claim isn't grounded only in the Qur'an."}},{"id":"int-05","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"can I believe both?","answer":"You can love both, learn from both, and keep thinking about what feels true to you as you grow. Some people follow two traditions, some choose one, some find their own way. Some religions ask people to choose, and you'll understand that more when you're older. Whatever you decide, we love you just the same.","grownup":"Traditions differ here: many Hindus readily honor Jesus, while Catholic, Muslim and traditional Jewish teaching generally ask for one exclusive commitment. Say this honestly, without guilt. Unconditional love is the answer your child most needs.","source":"General guidance (no single text); each tradition's own teachers should frame its view","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"int-06","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"why do we have two holidays?","answer":"Because we have two families' stories! Each holiday tells a story that's special to one side of our family. Diwali celebrates light, Christmas celebrates Jesus being born, and we get to share in both. It's like being invited to two birthday parties. Which one should we start getting ready for?","grownup":"Swap in your own holidays. Learn each one's meaning from the family that keeps it, and let each grandparent lead 'their' day. Keeping the stories distinct, not merged into one winter holiday, honors both. The light lesson pairs well.","source":"General guidance (no single text)","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"int-07","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"what happens when we die?","answer":"Different families believe different things. Many Hindus believe the soul is born again in a new life. Christians believe in being with God in heaven. It's okay to wonder about it, and even to feel a little scared. What do you think happens? And I'm right here with you.","grownup":"Give each tradition's answer with its owner (Bhagavad Gita 2.22; John 14:2), then return to safety: 'I'm here.' For a grieving child, a children's grief resource helps. If a child ever talks about wanting to die or self-harm, call or text 988 (US) or local emergency help.","source":"Bhagavad Gita 2.22 (paraphrase); John 14:2 (KJV)","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Removed 'nobody knows exactly', which undercuts what both traditions teach in faith; fairer to each parent."}},{"id":"int-08","path":"INTERFAITH","q":"will I have to choose?","answer":"Nobody is going to make you choose between the people you love. You can learn about both, ask questions, and take your time. When you're grown up, you'll decide what you believe, and we'll love you no matter what. For now, your job is to be curious, kind, and loved.","grownup":"Kids often fear choosing between parents. Parents may still choose rites for them (baptism, a naming, a sacred thread); explain these as family gifts, not final choices. Agree as a couple before answering, and shield the child from relatives' pressure.","source":"General guidance (no single text)","review":{"status":"pending"}}],"shared":[{"id":"shared-01-light","theme":"light","title":"a little light in the dark","hook":"It's dark by dinnertime now. One grandma is filling clay lamps with oil, the other is lighting the first purple candle on the Advent wreath, and your kid wants to know whose candles are the real ones.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Diwali: rows of diyas, rangoli at the door, fireworks and sweets; a lamp is also offered every day in aarti.","meaning":"Light over darkness and good over evil; in many homes, Rama's homecoming and welcoming Lakshmi.","source":"Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28 (paraphrase: lead me from darkness to light); regional Diwali traditions"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Hanukkah: one more light on the menorah each of eight nights, set where people passing can see it.","meaning":"Remembering the rededication of the Temple and the small jar of oil that lasted; making the miracle known.","source":"Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 21b (paraphrase); 1 Maccabees 4:36–59"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Advent wreath candles in the four weeks before Christmas; candlelight services on Christmas Eve.","meaning":"Waiting for Jesus, whom Christians call the light of the world.","source":"John 8:12 (KJV: 'I am the light of the world'); John 1:5"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"The Advent wreath (three purple candles and one rose); the Easter candle lit from new fire at the Easter Vigil; votive candles before the saints.","meaning":"Christ as light shining in darkness; a candle left burning as a prayer that keeps going.","source":"John 1:5 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Roman Missal, Easter Vigil"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Lanterns and lamps at Vesak; lamps offered at shrines.","meaning":"Awakening pictured as light; wisdom clearing away ignorance.","source":"Dhammapada 172 (Muller 1881, paraphrase: one who was careless and becomes mindful brightens the world like the moon freed from clouds)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Bandi Chhor Divas, on the same day as Diwali: the Harmandir Sahib (Golden Temple) is lit up and homes light diyas.","meaning":"Sikh tradition tells that Guru Hargobind, released from Gwalior Fort, asked that 52 imprisoned kings be freed with him.","source":"Sikh historical tradition, a traditional account whose details historians discuss (paraphrase)"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Not central as a festival: there's no lamp-lighting holiday. Light is a central image instead.","meaning":"The Qur'an calls Allah the Light of the heavens and the earth, and likens that light to a lamp in a niche.","source":"Qur'an 24:35 (Pickthall 1930: 'Allah is the Light of the heavens and the earth')"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"Lighting a candle on the longest night of the year, the winter solstice, or for someone you're thinking of.","meaning":"From that night on, the days grow longer; a small, deliberate act of hope.","source":"Seasonal observance (the solstice); no single text"}},"shared":"In each of these homes, a small flame is lit on purpose against the dark, and it means hope.","different":"The same flame tells different stories: Hanukkah remembers a historical rededication and a miracle, Diwali often celebrates Rama's homecoming and Lakshmi, and Advent waits for a birth.","familyActivity":"Turn off every light at dinner. Light one candle, and let each person name one bright spot from their day and one person who could use some light.","kidLine":"Lots of families light a little flame when it's dark, and each one tells a story about hope.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Dhammapada 172 paraphrase corrected (it is about one who was heedless and becomes heedful, not 'one who wakes up'). Bandi Chhor story labeled as a traditional telling. Diwali/Rama already framed as 'in many homes'. Qur'an 24:35 Pickthall wording confirmed."}},{"id":"shared-02-fasting","theme":"fasting","title":"going without, on purpose","hook":"One parent is up before dawn to eat before the Ramadan fast, the other is giving up chocolate for Lent, and your kid asks why grown-ups would skip food on purpose.","byPath":{"ISLAM":{"practice":"Ramadan: no food or drink from dawn to sunset for a month, then iftar together at sunset. Children, the sick and travelers are exempt.","meaning":"Mindfulness of God (taqwa), gratitude, and feeling the hunger of people who are poor.","source":"Qur'an 2:183–185 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Yom Kippur: no food or drink from sunset to nightfall the next day; also Tisha B'Av and several minor fasts.","meaning":"Afflicting the soul to turn back to God and to one another.","source":"Leviticus 16:29–31 (JPS 1917: 'ye shall afflict your souls'); Isaiah 58:6–7 on the fast God wants"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"Fasting on Ash Wednesday and Good Friday, no meat on Fridays of Lent, and a one-hour fast before Communion.","meaning":"Penance joined with prayer and almsgiving; a way of joining Christ's self-giving.","source":"Code of Canon Law 919, 1250–1252; Catechism 1438"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Lent, the forty days before Easter: many give something up; Orthodox Christians keep long food fasts; for many Protestants fasting is a personal choice.","meaning":"Following Jesus's forty days in the wilderness; making room for prayer and giving.","source":"Matthew 4:1–2; Matthew 6:16–18"},"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Vrat: fasting on days such as Ekadashi, Navratri or Mahashivaratri, sometimes eating only fruit or one meal.","meaning":"Self-discipline and devotion to a chosen deity; the Gita warns against extremes in either direction.","source":"Puranic and regional practice (paraphrase); Bhagavad Gita 6.16–17"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Monastics don't eat after midday; lay Buddhists may keep eight precepts, including that rule, on Uposatha days.","meaning":"Simplicity and a clear mind, not punishment; the Buddha turned away from extreme fasting and taught the middle way.","source":"Vinaya, Pacittiya 37 on eating at the wrong time (paraphrase); Anguttara Nikaya 3.70 (lay Uposatha precepts); Samyutta Nikaya 56.11 (the middle way)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Not central: Sikh teaching holds that ritual fasting isn't needed.","meaning":"The Gurus taught that remembering God, honest work and sharing matter more than going without; feeding others in langar is the practice.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 1136 (Guru Arjan, Raag Bhairao: I keep neither the fasts nor the month of Ramadan; I serve the One), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"A simple fast from something (screens, sugar, complaining) for a day or a week.","meaning":"Noticing what we lean on, and making room for what matters.","source":"Thoreau, Walden (1854), ch. 2 (paraphrase: simplify)"}},"shared":"Each of these fasts says going without for a while can wake up the heart, and often what's saved is given to someone hungry.","different":"Islam and Judaism command fasts on set days and Catholics have a few required days, while for many other Christians and many Hindus fasting is chosen; Sikh teaching sets ritual fasting aside altogether.","familyActivity":"Pick one small thing to go without together for one evening (dessert or screens, never meals for kids) and put what you'd have spent in a jar for someone who's hungry.","kidLine":"Some families skip food or treats for a while to feel close to God and to remember people who are hungry.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Ang 1136 confirmed via BaniDB ('varat na rahau na meh ramadhaanaa'). The line sets aside both Hindu vrat and Ramadan, so the paraphrase now shows both and the Guru's positive point, so Islam isn't the foil. Noon rule given its Vinaya number (Pacittiya 37). Canon 919, 1250-1252 and Catechism 1438 confirmed."}},{"id":"shared-03-giving","theme":"giving","title":"what's in your hands","hook":"The coin box by the door, the envelope at church, the cooks at the gurdwara, the money set aside at Eid: your kid notices that every grandparent gives something away, and wants to know why.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Dana: giving food, money or learning, especially on festivals and in memory of loved ones.","meaning":"Giving with faith and humility; the best gift is given because it's right, expecting nothing back.","source":"Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.3 (paraphrase: give with faith, give with modesty); Bhagavad Gita 17.20"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Tzedakah: a coin box at home, giving before Shabbat and holidays.","meaning":"From tzedek, justice: giving is what's owed, not only kindness; the highest level helps someone stand on their own.","source":"Deuteronomy 15:7–11; Maimonides, Mishneh Torah, Gifts to the Poor 10:7 (paraphrase)"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Offerings at church, food banks, giving to people in need.","meaning":"Jesus said that what is done for the least is done for him.","source":"Matthew 25:40 (KJV: 'Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me')"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"Almsgiving in Lent; the corporal works of mercy: feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, clothe the naked, welcome the stranger, visit the sick and imprisoned, bury the dead.","meaning":"Love for the poor as love for Christ.","source":"Matthew 25:35–40 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism 2447"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Zakat, a required yearly share of savings for set groups in need; sadaqah, voluntary giving at any time.","meaning":"Wealth is a trust from Allah, and giving purifies it.","source":"Qur'an 2:43, 9:60, 9:103 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Dana: giving food to monastics and to anyone in need.","meaning":"Giving is the first ground of merit and loosens the grip of greed.","source":"Itivuttaka 60 (paraphrase); Dhammapada 177 (Muller 1881, paraphrase: the wise rejoice in giving)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Dasvandh, giving a tenth of earnings; seva in the langar, cooking and serving anyone who comes.","meaning":"Vand chhakna, sharing what you have, is one of Guru Nanak's three pillars with remembering God and honest work.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 1245 (Guru Nanak: one who earns by honest work and gives some of it recognizes the way), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"Giving time, money or attention, and noticing who's been left out.","meaning":"Generosity as a practice that changes the giver.","source":"Seneca, On Benefits (De Beneficiis) 2.10 (paraphrase: the giver should forget the gift, the receiver remember it)"}},"shared":"Every one of these homes says that what we have isn't only ours, and that sharing it is part of being good.","different":"Zakat in Islam is a required share with set rules, and Jewish law also treats tzedakah as an obligation; in several other paths, giving is left more to each person's heart.","familyActivity":"Make a giving jar tonight. Everyone adds something (a coin, a toy to donate, a promise to help) and together you choose where it goes this month.","kidLine":"In all these families, sharing what's in your hands is part of loving God and loving people.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Itivuttaka 60 confirmed (three grounds of merit, giving first). Dhammapada 177 paraphrase made explicit. Seneca pinned to 2.10 ('the giver should at once forget, the receiver never'). Ang 1245 ('ghaal khai kichh hathahu dhei') confirmed via BaniDB."}},{"id":"shared-04-thanks-before-meals","theme":"giving thanks before meals","title":"before the first bite","hook":"At one grandparent's table nobody eats until grace is said; at the other, someone whispers bismillah, or a plate is offered to God first. Your kid just wants to know when they can eat.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Offering the food to God first (naivedya), then eating it as prasad; some families recite Bhagavad Gita 4.24 before meals.","meaning":"Food is a gift, and eating what's been offered keeps the meal sacred.","source":"Bhagavad Gita 4.24 and 3.13 (paraphrase)"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"The blessing over bread (hamotzi) before the meal, and Birkat Hamazon, grace after it.","meaning":"Blessing God for food; the Torah's own command is to bless after eating and being satisfied.","source":"Deuteronomy 8:10 (JPS 1917: 'And thou shalt eat and be satisfied, and bless the LORD thy God'); Mishnah Berakhot 6:1"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Saying grace before meals, in set or spontaneous words.","meaning":"Jesus gave thanks before breaking bread; gratitude for daily bread.","source":"Mark 6:41; Matthew 6:11"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"The sign of the cross and the traditional grace: 'Bless us, O Lord, and these thy gifts, which we are about to receive from thy bounty, through Christ our Lord. Amen.'","meaning":"Every meal received as a gift from God.","source":"Traditional Catholic table prayer (public domain)"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Saying bismillah before eating and alhamdulillah after; eating with the right hand.","meaning":"Remembering Allah at the start and thanking Him at the end.","source":"Sahih al-Bukhari 5376 (paraphrase); Qur'an 2:172 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Monastics reflect before eating that food is for sustaining the body, not for indulgence; many lay families pause in quiet.","meaning":"Mindful, grateful, unhurried eating.","source":"Sabbasava Sutta, Majjhima Nikaya 2 (paraphrase)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"In the langar, food is blessed through Ardas before it's served; at home, many families pause to remember Waheguru.","meaning":"Food is God's gift, shared equally.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (langar and Ardas), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"A pause before eating: each person names one thing they're grateful for, or thanks the people who grew and cooked the food.","meaning":"Noticing the many hands behind one meal.","source":"Marcus Aurelius, Meditations, Book 1 (Long 1862), a book of thanks (paraphrase)"}},"shared":"Every one of these tables stops for a breath before eating, to say that this food is a gift.","different":"In Judaism the blessing after the meal is the one the Torah itself commands, while most others give thanks mainly before; and some offer the food to God first, while others thank God for it.","familyActivity":"Tonight, let each side of the family teach its real grace or blessing, and let the youngest name one person who helped bring the food to the table.","kidLine":"Lots of families say thank you before they eat, in lots of different words.","review":{"status":"pending"}},{"id":"shared-05-guest","theme":"welcoming a guest","title":"set another plate","hook":"A friend's family is coming to dinner, and your kid asks why Nani has been cooking for three days. In many homes, a guest isn't a bother. A guest is an honor.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Offering water, the best seat and food first to the guest.","meaning":"Atithi devo bhava: treat the guest as God.","source":"Taittiriya Upanishad 1.11.2 (paraphrase)"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Hachnasat orchim, welcoming guests, especially at the Shabbat and Passover table.","meaning":"Abraham ran from his tent to welcome three strangers; early in the Seder, the Haggadah invites all who are hungry to come and eat.","source":"Genesis 18:1–8; Passover Haggadah (paraphrase)"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Hospitality to friends and strangers.","meaning":"Welcoming strangers may mean welcoming angels, and welcoming Christ.","source":"Hebrews 13:2 (KJV: 'Be not forgetful to entertain strangers: for thereby some have entertained angels unawares'); Matthew 25:35"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"Hospitality in homes, parishes and monasteries.","meaning":"Every guest received as Christ himself.","source":"Rule of St. Benedict, ch. 53 (paraphrase); Matthew 25:35"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Generous hosting: guests are fed and honored.","meaning":"Honoring the guest is a sign of faith.","source":"Sahih al-Bukhari 6018 (paraphrase: whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him be generous to his guest)"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Offering food and drink to visitors and to monastics.","meaning":"Hospitality is part of dana and of the duties between friends.","source":"Sigalovada Sutta, Digha Nikaya 31 (paraphrase)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Langar: anyone is fed, of any faith or background, seated together.","meaning":"Seeing God's light in everyone; no one is a stranger.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 1299 (Guru Arjan: no one is my enemy, no one a stranger), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"An open seat at the table, and inviting someone who might be alone.","meaning":"An old human value: in Homer's Odyssey, a stranger is fed before being asked their name.","source":"Homer, Odyssey, Book 1 (Butler 1900), paraphrase"}},"shared":"Every one of these homes says the person at your door deserves your best, and many say God is close when a guest is welcomed.","different":"Hindu teaching says to treat the guest as God, and Christians speak of meeting Christ in the guest; Islam and Judaism honor the guest as a sacred duty while keeping God and the person clearly distinct.","familyActivity":"Plan a guest night: choose together who to invite (a neighbor, a new classmate's family) and cook one dish from each side of the family.","kidLine":"In lots of families, a guest gets the best seat, because welcoming people matters to God.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Ha lachma anya comes at the start of Maggid, not the opening of the Seder. Bukhari 6018 wording is 'entertain his guest generously'; 'honor his guest' is the wording of other narrations. Ang 1299 ('naa ko bairee nahee bigaanaa') confirmed via BaniDB."}},{"id":"shared-06-new-baby","theme":"welcoming a new baby","title":"welcome, little one","hook":"A new cousin is born. One side is planning a baptism, the other a naming ceremony, and your kid asks what everyone did when they were born.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Jatakarma at birth and namkaran, the naming ceremony, often in the first weeks; in some families today, Om is traced on the baby's tongue with honey.","meaning":"Welcoming the new life and asking blessings for a long, good life.","source":"Grihya Sutras (jatakarma: the newborn is given a taste of honey and ghee), paraphrase; tracing Om with honey is a later family custom"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Brit milah on the eighth day for boys; a naming ceremony for girls (simchat bat or zeved habat).","meaning":"Joining the covenant of Abraham and receiving a Hebrew name.","source":"Genesis 17:12"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Infant baptism in many churches; a dedication service in churches that baptize later, when a person chooses.","meaning":"Welcoming the child into God's family and the church's care.","source":"Mark 10:14 (KJV: 'Suffer the little children to come unto me'); Matthew 28:19"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"Infant baptism with water and the sign of the cross, godparents, a white garment and a lit candle.","meaning":"New life in Christ and belonging to the Church.","source":"Catechism 1250–1255"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Many families whisper the call to prayer (adhan) in the baby's ear; the aqiqah, often on the seventh day, brings naming, a shared meal and charity.","meaning":"The first words a baby hears are about God; thanks shared with the community.","source":"Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Aqiqa (paraphrase); the adhan custom is reported in Sunan Abu Dawud and Jami at-Tirmidhi (paraphrase; scholars discuss its chain)"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"No single rite: in many countries families bring the baby to the temple for blessing chants (paritta) and sometimes a name from a monk.","meaning":"Protection and goodwill for a new life.","source":"Mangala Sutta and Karaniya Metta Sutta (Khuddakapatha 5 and 9), commonly chanted as paritta"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Naam karan at the gurdwara: the Guru Granth Sahib is opened at random, and the first letter of the hymn guides the name; Kaur or Singh is added.","meaning":"The child's name comes through the Guru.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (birth and naming ceremony), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"A welcome circle: family and friends each share a wish for the baby, maybe written in a book.","meaning":"Every child belongs to a whole circle of people.","source":"Gibran, The Prophet (1923), 'On Children' (paraphrase: children come through you but not from you)"}},"shared":"Every one of these families gathers around a new baby, says a name out loud with love, and promises to help this child grow.","different":"In some traditions a rite brings the baby into a covenant or church (brit milah, baptism); in others, the heart of the welcome is the name and a blessing.","familyActivity":"Get out your child's baby photos and tell the story of their name: who chose it, what it means, and who held them first.","kidLine":"When you were born, people who love you gathered around and gave you your name, one of your very first gifts.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: The Grihya Sutras describe feeding honey and ghee at jatakarma (and naming on the 10th or 12th day, varying by text); tracing Om on the tongue is a later custom, now labeled so. Adhan in the newborn's ear kept as 'many families' with the chain discussion flagged; a hadith scholar should confirm the framing."}},{"id":"shared-07-wedding","theme":"a wedding","title":"two families, one table","hook":"Your kid has been to a wedding with seven steps around a fire, and another where a glass was stomped underfoot. They want to know which one is the real way to get married.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Saptapadi, seven steps around the sacred fire, each with a vow; garlands exchanged.","meaning":"Friendship and partnership for life, with the fire as witness.","source":"Rig Veda 10.85 (the wedding hymn), paraphrase; Grihya Sutra rites"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"The chuppah canopy, the ketubah marriage contract, seven blessings, and breaking a glass.","meaning":"Building a home together, open on all sides; remembering sorrow even in great joy.","source":"Babylonian Talmud Ketubot 7b–8a (the seven blessings), paraphrase"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Vows before God and witnesses, often with a reading from 1 Corinthians 13.","meaning":"Two become one; love that is patient and kind.","source":"Genesis 2:24; 1 Corinthians 13:4 (KJV: 'Charity suffereth long, and is kind')"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"The sacrament of Matrimony, often within a Nuptial Mass; in the Latin Church the couple confer the sacrament on each other.","meaning":"A lifelong covenant reflecting Christ's love for the Church.","source":"Catechism 1601, 1623; Ephesians 5:25, 32"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Nikah: both give consent before witnesses, the groom gives the bride a mahr (marriage gift), and a walima feast follows.","meaning":"Marriage as rest, love and mercy between two people.","source":"Qur'an 30:21 and 4:4 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Not a religious sacrament: weddings follow local custom, often with monks invited to chant blessings.","meaning":"The Buddha gave advice on what spouses owe each other.","source":"Sigalovada Sutta, Digha Nikaya 31 (paraphrase)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Anand Karaj: the couple walk four times around the Guru Granth Sahib as the four laavan are sung.","meaning":"One light in two bodies; a shared journey toward God.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 773–774 (Guru Ram Das, the Laavan) and Ang 788 (Guru Amar Das: they are truly wed who are one light in two bodies), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"Couples often write their own vows and borrow readings from several traditions, naming each one.","meaning":"Togetherness with room to grow.","source":"Gibran, The Prophet (1923), 'On Marriage' (paraphrase: let there be spaces in your togetherness)"}},"shared":"Every one of these weddings makes promises out loud, in front of people who love the couple, and turns two families into one bigger family.","different":"Each path frames the promise its own way: a sacrament for Catholics, a covenant for many Protestants, kiddushin (making holy) for Jews, a sacred contract (nikah) for Muslims, a samskara for Hindus, a vow before the Guru for Sikhs; in Buddhism, marriage is a family and civil matter that monks may bless.","familyActivity":"Bring out wedding photos, yours or the grandparents', and let each person tell one tradition from their wedding and what it meant to them.","kidLine":"At every wedding, two people make big promises to love each other, and their families become one.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: The old 'different' line singled out Catholics and Sikhs as sacred 'in a particular way', which implied the Hindu, Jewish and Muslim rites were less so; now every path is named fairly. Ang 773-774 (Laavan) and Ang 788 ('ek jot dhui mooratee') confirmed via BaniDB."}},{"id":"shared-08-remembering","theme":"remembering the dead","title":"the people we miss","hook":"It's the anniversary of a grandparent's death. One side lights a candle and prays, the other cooks the foods he loved. Your kid asks if Grandpa can see them.","byPath":{"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Shraddha rites, especially during Pitru Paksha: offering food and water to ancestors and feeding people in their name.","meaning":"Gratitude and care for the ancestors.","source":"Garuda Purana (paraphrase); Bhagavad Gita 2.22"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Shiva, seven days of mourning at home; Kaddish; a yahrzeit candle each year; small stones left on graves.","meaning":"Keeping memory alive; the Kaddish praises God rather than speaking of death.","source":"Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1 (paraphrase); Shulchan Aruch, Yoreh De'ah, laws of mourning"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Funerals and memorial services, visiting graves, remembering on All Saints' Day.","meaning":"Grieving with hope in the resurrection.","source":"1 Thessalonians 4:13; John 11:25 (KJV: 'I am the resurrection, and the life')"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"Masses offered for the dead, All Souls Day on November 2, praying the rosary for loved ones.","meaning":"Love continues; prayers help the dead on their way to God.","source":"2 Maccabees 12:46 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism 1032"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"The janazah prayer and a prompt burial; prayers (dua) and charity on behalf of the person.","meaning":"We belong to Allah and to Him we return.","source":"Qur'an 2:156 (Pickthall 1930: 'Lo! we are Allah's and lo! unto Him we are returning')"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Chanting, giving to monks and dedicating the merit to the person who died; festivals such as Obon in Japan.","meaning":"Kindness done in their name, and accepting that all things change.","source":"Tirokudda Kanda, Khuddakapatha 7 (paraphrase)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Kirtan, Ardas and a reading of the Guru Granth Sahib after the cremation.","meaning":"Accepting God's will (hukam) and singing praise rather than wailing.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (funeral ceremonies), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"Telling stories, cooking the person's favorite food, a memory table with photos.","meaning":"The people we love stay part of who we are.","source":"Marcus Aurelius, Meditations, Book 1 (Long 1862): naming what each person taught him (paraphrase)"}},"shared":"Every one of these families keeps saying the names of the people they miss, and does something good in their memory.","different":"Hindus, Sikhs and Buddhists speak of rebirth, each in its own way, while Jews, Christians and Muslims speak of resurrection or the world to come; and Catholics and Orthodox Christians pray for the dead, while many Protestants don't.","familyActivity":"Light a candle, set out a photo, and let each person tell one small story about someone who has died. If no one close has, tell a story about a great-grandparent.","kidLine":"When we miss someone, we say their name, tell their stories, and do kind things for them.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Sikhs added to the rebirth paths; Orthodox added; 'most Protestants' softened to 'many'."}},{"id":"shared-09-rest","theme":"a day of rest","title":"a day to stop","hook":"One grandparent won't pick up the phone on Friday night; the other says Sunday is for church and a long lunch. Your kid asks why the family calendar has stop days.","byPath":{"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Shabbat, from Friday sunset to Saturday nightfall: candles, wine and challah, no work, time with family and rest.","meaning":"God rested on the seventh day; rest as a gift and as freedom.","source":"Genesis 2:2–3; Exodus 20:8–11; Deuteronomy 5:12–15"},"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Sunday, the Lord's Day: worship and, for many, rest; a few churches keep Saturday as the Sabbath.","meaning":"Remembering Jesus's resurrection on the first day of the week.","source":"Acts 20:7; Mark 2:27 (KJV: 'The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath')"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"Sunday Mass is an obligation, with rest from unnecessary work.","meaning":"The day of resurrection, kept holy.","source":"Catechism 2180–2185; Code of Canon Law 1247"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Jumu'ah, the Friday congregational prayer at midday.","meaning":"Leaving business to gather and remember Allah, then going back out to work: a day of gathering more than a day of rest.","source":"Qur'an 62:9–10 (Pickthall 1930, paraphrase)"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Uposatha, observance days at the new and full moon (and in many places the quarter moons): visiting the temple and keeping extra precepts.","meaning":"A day to renew practice and live more simply.","source":"Muluposatha Sutta, Anguttara Nikaya 3.70 (paraphrase)"},"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Not central: there's no weekly Sabbath. Many keep days for particular deities (Monday for Shiva, Tuesday for Hanuman) and pray daily.","meaning":"Sacred time is woven through each day and the festival calendar.","source":"Regional practice (no single text)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Not central: there's no weekly holy day. Daily prayers (Nitnem) morning and evening; many gurdwaras outside India gather on Sundays for convenience.","meaning":"Remembering God in every day, while working and living.","source":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (Nitnem), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"A screen-free evening or a slow Sunday: a walk, a meal, nothing to finish.","meaning":"Unhurried time as nourishment.","source":"Thoreau, Walden (1854), ch. 2 (paraphrase: living deliberately)"}},"shared":"Many of these families mark a time each week, or each moon, to put work down and turn toward God, each other, or the quiet.","different":"Shabbat is a commanded day of rest from work, while Jumu'ah is a gathering for prayer after which the Qur'an says people may go back out and seek their living; Hindus and Sikhs keep no weekly holy day at all.","familyActivity":"Choose one stop hour this week: phones in a basket, a meal or a walk together, and each person says one thing they're glad to rest from.","kidLine":"Some families have a special time to stop working and just be together.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Qur'an 62:10 is generally read as permission to return to work, not a command; softened. Canon 1247 and Catechism 2180-2185 confirmed."}},{"id":"shared-10-golden-rule","theme":"the golden rule","title":"the rule in every kitchen","hook":"Your kids are fighting over the last cookie. One grandma quotes Jesus, the other quotes the Mahabharata, and they're saying nearly the same thing.","byPath":{"CHRISTIANITY":{"practice":"Treating others the way you'd want to be treated.","meaning":"Jesus says that this is the law and the prophets.","source":"Matthew 7:12 (KJV: 'Therefore all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them: for this is the law and the prophets')"},"CATHOLIC":{"practice":"The same teaching, joined to the command to love your neighbor as yourself.","meaning":"The Catechism lists the golden rule among the rules that apply in every case.","source":"Luke 6:31; Mark 12:31 (Douay-Rheims, paraphrase); Catechism 1789"},"JUDAISM":{"practice":"Love your neighbor as yourself; Hillel's summary: what is hateful to you, don't do to your neighbor.","meaning":"Hillel called it the whole Torah, with the rest its commentary: go and learn.","source":"Leviticus 19:18 (JPS 1917: 'thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself: I am the LORD'); Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 31a (paraphrase)"},"ISLAM":{"practice":"Wanting for others what you want for yourself.","meaning":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ tied it to true faith.","source":"Sahih al-Bukhari 13; Sahih Muslim 45 (paraphrase: none of you truly believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself)"},"HINDUISM":{"practice":"Not doing to others what you would find painful yourself.","meaning":"The Mahabharata calls this, in brief, dharma.","source":"Mahabharata, Anushasana Parva 113.8 in the Pune critical edition (114.8 in some other editions), paraphrase"},"BUDDHISM":{"practice":"Comparing yourself with others, and so harming no one.","meaning":"Everyone fears pain and loves life, just as you do.","source":"Dhammapada 129–130 (Muller 1881, paraphrase)"},"SIKHISM":{"practice":"Seeing no one as an enemy or a stranger, and serving all.","meaning":"The same divine light is in everyone.","source":"Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 1299 (Guru Arjan: no one is my enemy, no one a stranger), paraphrase"},"SPIRITUAL":{"practice":"Reciprocity: don't do to others what you wouldn't want done to you.","meaning":"Confucius named it the one word to live by.","source":"Confucius, Analects 15.23 (Legge 1861), paraphrase"}},"shared":"Every one of these paths asks you to imagine being the other person, and to treat them the way you'd want to be treated.","different":"Hillel and Confucius say it as don't do what hurts, while Jesus says it as do the good you'd want; and each path grounds it differently, in God's command, in one light in all, or in shared human feeling.","familyActivity":"Play switch seats: when a squabble starts this week, the two kids trade places and say how the other one feels. Then ask each grandparent for their tradition's version of the rule.","kidLine":"Lots of religions have a rule like this, each in its own words: treat people the way you'd want to be treated.","review":{"status":"pending","notes":"Fact-check 2026-10-14: Meanings tightened to what the texts say (Matthew 7:12 'this is the law and the prophets'; CCC 1789 'some rules apply in every case'; Mahabharata 'this in brief is dharma'). Edition numbering for the Mahabharata verse given. kidLine no longer says the religions say 'the same' thing. Bukhari 13 / Muslim 45 and Shabbat 31a confirmed."}}]};
